lar-8f\" d=\"M 2163 397 L 1677 397 L 1677 1734 L 429 1734 L 429 2170 L 1677 2170 L 1677 3507 L 2163 3507 L 2163 2170 L 3411 2170 L 3411 1734 L 2163 1734 L 2163 397 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-8f\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" transform=\"translate(120 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3e\" transform=\"translate(147.203125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-40\" transform=\"translate(207.203125 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_15\"><g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(255.663417 15.4) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-90\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" transform=\"translate(120 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3c\" transform=\"translate(147.203125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3d\" transform=\"translate(207.203125 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_16\"><g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(324.034958 31.000427) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3b\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-38\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" transform=\"translate(120 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-38\" transform=\"translate(147.203125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3b\" transform=\"translate(207.203125 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pedff8dfd4b\"><rect x=\"25\" y=\"10.472\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"149.688\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · From 3PL cost to own-fleet cost ($M a year)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Empty miles: 35% × 4.4M = 1.54M, costing $1.46M. Own fleet: <b>$11.24M</b>, now <b>$0.68M more</b> than the 3PL.</p> <p>If BlueTide sells half its return capacity to other shippers at $1.60 a mile, it earns $1.23M: net cost $10.01M, $0.55M cheaper than the 3PL.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> on transport cost alone, owning saves only about 5%, and only if half the return trips are filled. That is a thin, fragile margin.</p>"},{"q":"BlueTide's own trucks would carry temperature sensors with live alerts, cutting spoilage to about 0.5%. What does that change?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Spoilage saving: $150M × (1.2% − 0.5%) = <b>$1.05M a year</b>, more than any difference in transport cost.</p> <p>Total yearly gain from owning: <b>$1.60M</b>. Cash payback on the $7.2M fleet: $7.2M ÷ ($1.60M gain + $1.2M non-cash depreciation) = <b>2.6 years</b>.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> the case for owning is about product quality, not trucking cost. That raises a question: could the 3PL fit the same sensors for less?</p>"},{"q":"What would you recommend, and what would you check first?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li>Ask the 3PL to commit to sensor monitoring and a spoilage penalty in its contract. If it agrees, most of the gain comes without owning trucks.</li> <li>If not, build a fleet for the core, predictable routes and keep the 3PL for peaks and long-haul.</li> <li>Check: the 1.2% spoilage figure, how reliably return loads can be sold, and driver availability.</li></ul>"}],"reco":"On trucking cost alone, owning a fleet saves only about 5% once empty return trips are counted, and only if BlueTide can sell half its return capacity. The real prize is spoilage: better temperature control saves about $1.05M a year. First, ask the 3PL to fit sensors and accept a spoilage penalty, which captures most of that gain without $7.2M of trucks. If it won't, buy a fleet for the core routes and keep the 3PL for peaks (a full 40-truck fleet would gain about $1.60M a year, cash payback 2.6 years; a smaller fleet scales this down). Risk: running a fleet is a new skill. Next step: verify spoilage by route.","great":["Builds the own-fleet cost line by line","Catches the empty-return-mile trap","Finds that spoilage outweighs trucking cost","Looks for a way to get the benefit without the asset"],"id":54,"slug":"bluetide-seafood"}